  <h2>Components to understand</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Springs and dampers:</strong> support vehicle weight and control body movement.</li>
    <li><strong>Control arms, links and bushings:</strong> locate the wheels while allowing suspension movement.</li>
    <li><strong>Ball joints and wheel bearings:</strong> allow steering/suspension articulation and wheel rotation.</li>
    <li><strong>Anti-roll components:</strong> connect suspension movement across an axle and influence body roll.</li>
    <li><strong>Steering rack, tie rods and joints:</strong> transfer steering input to the front wheels.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Configuration differences</h2>
  <p>Standard chassis, sport-oriented packages, wheel/tire combinations, and Polestar Engineered hardware are not interchangeable assumptions. Polestar Engineered vehicles use performance-specific suspension equipment. Confirm the actual damper, spring, bushing, arm, steering and alignment specification by VIN and option code before ordering parts.</p>
  <h2>Factory inspection interval</h2>
  <p>Volvo Car USA's combined 2020/2021 ICE and PHEV maintenance sheet lists front- and rear-suspension wear inspections at <strong>40,000-mile intervals</strong> (40,000, 80,000, 120,000 miles, and so on). This is an inspection item—not an instruction to replace dampers, arms, links or bushings automatically. The same sheet calls for related checks of driveshaft joints, rubber boots, and propeller-shaft components on applicable AWD models.</p>
  <p>Ask the technician to document the actual finding: torn boot, measurable play, damaged bushing, leaking damper, loose fastener, corrosion or abnormal tire wear. “Suspension service recommended” by itself does not identify the failed part.</p>
  <h2>Inspection symptoms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Clunks over bumps, squeaks, knocking, or repeated impact noises.</li>
    <li>Uneven tire wear, steering pull, vibration, or a steering wheel that is no longer centered.</li>
    <li>Visible spring damage, leaking dampers, torn boots, damaged bushings, or loose hardware.</li>
    <li>New steering effort, play, binding, or warning messages.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Record the road conditions, speed, steering angle, temperature, tire pressures, and whether the noise occurs over one-wheel or both-wheel bumps. Do not crawl beneath a vehicle supported only by a jack.</p>
  <h2>Useful diagnostic notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>For a clunk, record whether it occurs over a sharp bump, a slow driveway transition, braking, acceleration or steering input.</li>
    <li>For a vibration, note vehicle speed, road surface, whether it changes under braking, and whether it is felt through the steering wheel or seat.</li>
    <li>For a pull or uneven tire wear, record cold tire pressures, tire sizes, recent impacts and whether the steering wheel is centered.</li>
    <li>After suspension work, confirm whether a wheel alignment or steering-angle calibration is required by the specific repair procedure.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note"><strong>Safety:</strong> steering and suspension repairs affect vehicle control. Use the correct lift points, rated supports, one-time-use fasteners, torque sequence and alignment procedure from Volvo service information. If steering behavior changes suddenly, stop driving when safe and arrange inspection.</div>
